  // --- Handbook ---
  "game:handbook-category-prosequor": "Prosequor",
  "handbook-welcome-title": "Welcome to Prosequor",
  "handbook-welcome-text": "<strong>Welcome to Prosequor</strong><br>Prosequor is how you grow on this world. You get better by doing the work — digging, cooking, tending animals, and everything in between.<br><br>This primer is a short tour. Use Next and Previous at the bottom of each page, or pick a topic from the list.<ul><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-attributes\">Attributes</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-levels\">Skill levels</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-professions\">Professions</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-types\">Other skill types</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-points\">Skill points</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-quality\">Quality</a></li><li><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-friendliness\">Animal friendliness</a></li></ul><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-attributes\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-attributes-title": "Attributes",
  "handbook-attributes-text": "<strong>Attributes</strong><br>You have five attributes. Most Seraphs start near 10. Classes nudge those starting values and they all max at 18.<ul><li>Strength — melee hits, how much armor slows you, and later extra carrying room</li><li>Perception — bow and spear handling; later, better eyes in the dark</li><li>Constitution — health, how much you can eat, and later how slowly hunger returns</li><li>Inconspicuity — how soon animals notice you; later, luck with vessels and critical hits</li><li>Resilience — weather, frost, and falling; later, a chance to survive a blow that should have killed you</li></ul>The work you do feeds related attributes. When your player level hits certain milestones, the attribute you have been exercising the most goes up by one. Watch the ticks on the Stats panel — they show which attributes are closest to growing.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-welcome\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-levels\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-skill-levels-title": "Skill levels",
  "handbook-skill-levels-text": "<strong>Skill levels</strong><br>Every skill starts at 0. Do the activity and it gains experience. When a skill levels up, that progress also adds toward your player level.<ul><li>Player level runs from 1 to 50</li><li>Specialization skills can reach 100</li><li>Minor and passive skills cap at 50</li><li>Hobby skills cap at 20</li></ul>Your player level is not a separate grind. Level a few skills deeply or many skills a little — both raise the same player level.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-attributes\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-professions\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-professions-title": "Professions",
  "handbook-professions-text": "<strong>Professions</strong><br>A Profession is a specialization on a skill tree — Farmhand, Miner, Lumberjack, and the like. They typically open around skill level 30.<ul><li>It costs a specialization slot, not just a skill point</li><li>You earn one specialization slot every 10 player levels</li><li>Slots are shared across all skills, so choose with care</li><li>A profession usually boosts experience from that skill's work</li></ul>You do not need a profession to use a skill. It is a commitment that makes that path more rewarding.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-levels\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-types\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-skill-types-title": "Minor, passive, and hobby skills",
  "handbook-skill-types-text": "<strong>Minor, passive, and hobby skills</strong><br>Not every skill is a profession path.<ul><li>Minor skills have an unlock tree but no profession node. They cap at 50 and spend ordinary skill points.</li><li>Passive skills have no tree. They still level from use and give a steady bonus. Forager, Athletics, Swimming, Sneaking, and Adaptation work this way.</li><li>Hobby skills cap at 20. Their unlocks spend hobby points earned by leveling that hobby, not your shared skill points.</li></ul>Open the Skills tab in the character menu. Skills are grouped into Specializations, Minor Skills, Hobby Skills, and Passives. Empty groups stay hidden.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-professions\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-points\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-skill-points-title": "Skill points",
  "handbook-skill-points-text": "<strong>Skill points</strong><br>Skill points buy unlocks on skill trees (except hobbies).<br><br>You gain them in two ways:<ul><li>One point every time your player level goes up</li><li>One point for every 20 levels in a single skill</li></ul>Hobby unlocks use a separate pool that grows as that hobby levels. Specialization slots are also separate — they only buy professions.<br><br>Unspent points wait in the character Skills header until you spend them.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-types\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-quality\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-quality-title": "Quality",
  "handbook-quality-text": "<strong>Quality</strong><br>Some things you make can come out better than others. That grade is Quality.<br><br>You will see it at the bottom of an item tooltip: Nice, Fine, Superior, Excellent, or Masterful.<ul><li>Cooking, brewing, smithing, and many other crafts can roll quality</li><li>Higher skill, and certain unlocks, make better grades more likely</li><li>A better grade can mean a better meal, a stronger drink, or extra properties on the item</li><li>When you distill, the mash's quality carries into the spirit</li></ul>Quality is not a separate skill. It is the mark of a well-made thing.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-skill-points\">Previous</a> · <a href=\"handbook://prosequor-friendliness\">Next</a>",
  "handbook-friendliness-title": "Animal friendliness",
  "handbook-friendliness-text": "<strong>Animal friendliness</strong><br>Animals remember how you treat them. Friendliness is that memory.<ul><li>Feeding at a trough sometimes raises it. Husbandry unlocks can make those meals count more often.</li><li>An animal that wanders into your crops and eats them sometimes grows a little friendlier with the person who planted them.</li><li>Food you leave on the ground can do the same for whoever dropped it.</li><li>With Animal Whisperer, you can pet an animal that already likes you, and that can raise it too.</li><li>An animal needs a rest between improvements — you cannot rush it in one sitting.</li><li>Hitting an animal crashes its friendliness to the edge of friendly, and a further hit drops it to nothing. If you were its Favorite Seraph, that bond is gone.</li><li>Once friendliness is high enough, the animal is friendly: it fears you less, husbandry bonuses apply, and it may show a Favorite Seraph.</li></ul>Friendliness is not printed as a number. You will notice it in calmer animals, easier care, and that favorite-seraph line.<br><br><a href=\"handbook://prosequor-quality\">Previous</a>"
}
